dsv41 patch 0049 (20.10.2026): exl3_router prefetch depth and x staging as build flags (vllm_exl3_moe plugin only).
Profile of 19.10.2026 (1 stream): the router GEMV ([384, 5120] bf16 gate, one warp an expert) costs 23.6 us a layer past its predecessor
on the critical path (40 a round, ~0.9 ms), although its gate is in L2 by then (patch 0017's "moe" prefetch fork pulls
hc_ffn_fn + gate first, ~25 us before the router starts). The kernel keeps PF = 4 16-byte loads in flight a lane (five
dependent rounds over the 20 chunks of a row) and stages x after the PDL wait one load at a time. ROUTER_PF sets the
prefetch depth, ROUTER_XS the number of x loads a thread issues before storing them to shared memory. Neither changes
the arithmetic (each lane still adds its chunks in chunk order, the warp butterfly is unchanged): the logits keep their
bits, which a GPU benchmark checks (int32 views against the default build) while timing each variant
with the gate warm in L2 and cold. If warm and cold time alike, the router is latency-bound and the flags help; if the
in-serve time matches "cold", the gate is not L2-resident at the router and the prefetch order is the lever instead.
Switch: EXL3_ROUTER_FLAGS="-DROUTER_PF=<n> -DROUTER_XS=<n>" (part of the JIT hash; pre-build before a serve start).
Defaults (PF 4, XS 1) = the kernel as before.
Measured (20.10.2026): no flag combination built faster than the default, so we run the default build.

--- a/vllm_exl3_moe/csrc/exl3_router.cu
+++ b/vllm_exl3_moe/csrc/exl3_router.cu
@@ -34,7 +34,18 @@
 
 constexpr int WARPS = 8;                 // expert rows a block, one warp each
 constexpr int MAXM = 8;                  // rows (decode sizes; x is staged whole in shared memory)
-constexpr int PF = 4;                    // 16-byte row loads in flight a lane
+// dsv41 patch 0049: the prefetch depth and the x staging batch are build flags (EXL3_ROUTER_FLAGS="-DROUTER_PF=20
+// -DROUTER_XS=8"; the JIT hash includes the flags). Neither changes the arithmetic: every lane still adds its row's
+// 16-byte chunks in chunk order (j = it * 32 + lane, it ascending) and the warp reduces by the same butterfly, so the
+// logits keep their bits; only how many loads are in flight changes. Defaults = the kernel as before.
+#ifndef ROUTER_PF
+#define ROUTER_PF 4
+#endif
+#ifndef ROUTER_XS
+#define ROUTER_XS 1
+#endif
+constexpr int PF = ROUTER_PF;            // 16-byte row loads in flight a lane
+constexpr int XS = ROUTER_XS;            // x staging: 16-byte loads a thread issues before storing them
 constexpr int MAXN = 384;                // experts the fused top-k handles (a multiple of 32)
 constexpr int MAXK = 8;                  // top-k
 
@@ -95,9 +106,28 @@
         pre[i] = j < K8 ? ld_stream(wr + j) : make_uint4(0, 0, 0, 0);
     }
     exl3c::griddep_wait();
-    for (int i = threadIdx.x; i < M * K8; i += blockDim.x) {
-        const int m = i / K8, j = i - m * K8;
-        xs[i] = reinterpret_cast<const uint4*>(x + (size_t) m * ldx)[j];
+    if constexpr (XS <= 1) {
+        for (int i = threadIdx.x; i < M * K8; i += blockDim.x) {
+            const int m = i / K8, j = i - m * K8;
+            xs[i] = reinterpret_cast<const uint4*>(x + (size_t) m * ldx)[j];
+        }
+    } else {
+        for (int i0 = threadIdx.x; i0 < M * K8; i0 += XS * blockDim.x) {
+            uint4 v[XS];
+#pragma unroll
+            for (int u = 0; u < XS; ++u) {
+                const int i = i0 + u * blockDim.x;
+                if (i < M * K8) {
+                    const int m = i / K8, j = i - m * K8;
+                    v[u] = reinterpret_cast<const uint4*>(x + (size_t) m * ldx)[j];
+                }
+            }
+#pragma unroll
+            for (int u = 0; u < XS; ++u) {
+                const int i = i0 + u * blockDim.x;
+                if (i < M * K8) xs[i] = v[u];
+            }
+        }
     }
     __syncthreads();
 
